window.QA_KB={"about": [{"k": ["who", "huzaifa", "about you", "yourself", "introduce", "detail about", "tell me about", "profile"], "a": "Huzaifa Aamish is a Senior SQA Engineer with 7+ years across Android, iOS and web, on both client projects and long-running products. He leads a seven-member QA team on a telecom platform (calling, eSIM, recharge, payments) and works with AI for testing and for building tools like this site.\n\nCore strengths: functional and UI testing, analytics event validation (Mixpanel, Customer.io, Adjust), deep link and deferred deep link verification, payment flows (wallet, MoneyHash, Stripe, sandbox), in-app and push notifications, onboarding flows, test cases, use cases and flow diagrams, requirement vs Figma conflict detection, documentation, bug tracking tools, team and project management, and AI services such as chatbots, search and data analysis.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["analytic", "mixpanel", "customer.io", "customerio", "adjust", "event", "tracking", "attribution", "funnel"], "a": "Yes, analytics testing is one of Huzaifa's specialities. He validates events, properties and user profiles in Mixpanel, campaign triggers and journeys in Customer.io, and install attribution and deep link tracking in Adjust. Method: event dictionary review, capture at the wire (Charles Proxy), then verification in each dashboard, so product decisions are based on data that is actually correct.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["deep link", "deeplink", "deferred", "universal link", "app link", "url scheme"], "a": "Deep links are a strong area. Huzaifa verifies standard deep links, universal links (iOS) and app links (Android), and deferred deep links where the user installs first and lands on the right screen after. Covers cold start, warm start, logged in vs logged out, and attribution in Adjust.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["calling", "call", "esim", "recharge", "top up", "topup", "telecom", "voip", "sim"], "a": "Huzaifa is an expert in telecom product QA: outbound and inbound calling, call quality and routing, eSIM purchase, activation and profile lifecycle, and recharge and top-up flows end to end, including failure and retry paths. This is his daily work on a live operator platform.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["payment", "wallet", "moneyhash", "stripe", "sandbox", "checkout", "card", "refund", "transaction"], "a": "Payment testing is a core skill. Huzaifa has tested wallet balance flows, MoneyHash and Stripe integrations, and full sandbox to production verification: successful payments, declines, 3DS, timeouts, duplicate submissions, refunds and reconciliation between app, gateway and backend.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["ui test", "ui testing", "user interface", "visual", "design match", "pixel", "layout"], "a": "Yes, UI testing is part of every cycle. Huzaifa checks screens against Figma for spacing, typography, states (empty, loading, error), dark mode, localisation and device sizes, and files precise UI defects with side-by-side evidence.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["functional", "functionality", "feature test", "business logic"], "a": "Functional testing is his foundation: every feature verified against requirements and acceptance criteria, positive and negative paths, edge cases and data variations, across Android, iOS and web.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["documentation", "document", "test report", "release note", "sop", "write up", "wiki"], "a": "Huzaifa is strong in documentation: test plans, test strategies, test summary and release reports, ATP documents, SOPs and process wikis in Confluence. Clear enough for a new joiner to follow without a call.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["bug tracking", "bug tracker", "jira expert", "defect tool", "tracking tool", "issue tracker", "clickup", "trello expert"], "a": "He is an expert in bug tracking tools, mainly Jira (workflows, boards, dashboards, JQL filters, custom fields) and also Trello and ClickUp. He sets up defect workflows that teams actually follow.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["team management", "manage team", "team lead", "leadership", "mentor", "manage people"], "a": "Team management is a proven strength. Huzaifa leads a seven-member QA team: task allocation, daily syncs, quality gates, performance feedback and mentoring juniors into independent testers.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["project management", "manage project", "delivery", "timeline", "planning", "stakeholder", "coordination"], "a": "Huzaifa handles QA project management end to end: scoping, estimation, test schedules aligned with sprints, risk tracking, stakeholder updates and release readiness calls with product and engineering.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["test case", "use case", "flow diagram", "flowchart", "scenario", "test design", "user flow"], "a": "Expert level. Huzaifa writes structured test cases and use cases, and draws flow diagrams for complex journeys (onboarding, payments, eSIM activation) so gaps are visible before development starts.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["figma", "requirement conflict", "requirement gap", "design conflict", "mismatch", "prd", "specification"], "a": "One of his most valued skills: finding conflicts between requirements and Figma designs before coding starts. Missing states, contradictory rules, undefined error paths, all raised in refinement so they never become bugs.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["notification", "push", "in-app", "in app", "fcm", "apns", "alert", "banner"], "a": "Huzaifa is experienced in in-app and push notification testing: FCM and APNs delivery, permission states, foreground vs background vs killed app, deep link on tap, badge counts, and campaign triggers from Customer.io.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["onboarding", "signup", "sign up", "registration", "kyc", "first launch", "welcome"], "a": "Onboarding is a flow he tests in depth: sign-up, OTP verification, KYC steps, permissions, first-run tutorials and the analytics that measure drop-off at each step.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["ai service", "chatbot", "build chatbot", "search function", "data analy", "ai product", "ai solution", "ai tool"], "a": "Beyond QA, Huzaifa provides AI services: custom chatbots (like the one you are talking to), search functionality for apps and sites, data analysis and reporting automation, and AI-assisted test generation. Built with AI, reviewed by a human.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["service", "offer", "do you do", "help me with", "what can you", "expertise", "skills"], "a": "Services:\n- Mobile app testing (Android, iOS, real devices)\n- Web and API testing\n- Test automation (Appium, Selenium, Playwright)\n- Telecom QA (calling, SMS, OTP, eSIM, recharge)\n- Payments and analytics validation\n- QA process, documentation and team setup\n- AI services: chatbots, search, data analysis\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["experience", "years", "how long", "background", "career"], "a": "7+ years in QA. Domains: telecom (calling, eSIM, recharge, A2P SMS, OTP), enterprise HR (HRMS), construction tech (RoofScope), e-commerce (PartsGeek). Currently QA Team Lead with seven engineers.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["hire", "recruit", "job", "full time", "full-time", "remote", "open to", "position", "role", "join"], "a": "Yes, open to senior QA and quality engineering roles, remote or hybrid. Best first step: email huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com with the role and stack, you will get a reply within a day.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["freelance", "project basis", "upwork", "fiverr", "utest", "contract", "short term"], "a": "Freelance QA is available for project work: test cycles, automation, app store readiness. Active on Upwork, Fiverr and uTest, or direct via email and WhatsApp.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["rate", "price", "cost", "charge", "budget", "pricing", "fee", "how much"], "a": "Pricing depends on scope, platform count and duration. Send a one-paragraph brief to huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com and you will get a quote with a suggested test plan.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["contact", "email", "phone", "whatsapp", "reach", "linkedin", "get in touch"], "a": "Email: huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com\nPhone / WhatsApp: +92 303 9042017\nLinkedIn: linkedin.com/in/huzaifa-aamish\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["available", "availability", "start", "when can", "how soon"], "a": "Usually able to start a new project within a week. For full-time roles, notice period is discussed on the first call.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["tool", "stack", "technolog", "postman", "jira", "appium", "selenium", "playwright", "charles", "testrail"], "a": "Main tools: Postman and Newman for API, Appium and Selenium (Playwright where it fits) for automation, Jira for defects, Charles Proxy for traffic and analytics capture, Mixpanel, Customer.io and Adjust dashboards, Figma for design review, Confluence for documentation, plus AI tools for test design and log triage.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["this website", "this site", "built", "how was this", "made this"], "a": "This website was designed and coded with AI from a blank page by Huzaifa, then tested like any other product. It is a live example of the AI services he offers.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["hrms", "aramis", "roofscope", "wadic", "partsgeek", "project", "portfolio", "work", "case stud"], "a": "Selected work: national SIM registry design (West Africa), A2P SMS honeypot (Sahel), voice firewall acceptance (Southeast Asia), VoIP and eSIM platform QA, HRMS product by Aramis Enterprise Solutions, RoofScope for Wadic, and PartsGeek e-commerce. See the Selected work wheel on this page.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}, {"k": ["cv", "resume", "download", "pdf"], "a": "CV is available on request, email huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com and it will be sent the same day.\n\nWant your product bug-free and winning? Reach Huzaifa at huzaifa.aamish@gmail.com or +92 303 9042017."}], "topics": {"Selenium": [["What is Selenium?", "Selenium is an open-source suite for automating web browsers. Its core, Selenium WebDriver, drives real browsers through language bindings like Java, Python, C# and JavaScript."], ["What are the components of Selenium?", "Selenium WebDriver (browser automation API), Selenium IDE (record and playback browser extension), and Selenium Grid (parallel runs across machines and browsers)."], ["What is Selenium WebDriver?", "WebDriver is the API that sends commands to a browser driver (ChromeDriver, GeckoDriver), which controls the real browser. It is the part you write tests against."], ["What is Selenium Grid?", "Grid runs tests in parallel on multiple machines, browsers and OS combinations through a hub and nodes, cutting suite time and adding cross-browser coverage."], ["What is Selenium IDE?", "A browser extension for recording and replaying interactions. Good for quick prototypes, not for maintainable suites."], ["Which languages does Selenium support?", "Java, Python, C#, JavaScript, Ruby and Kotlin have official bindings."], ["Which browsers does Selenium support?", "Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari and any browser with a WebDriver-compliant driver."], ["What are Selenium locators?", "Ways to find elements: id, name, className, tagName, linkText, partialLinkText, cssSelector and xpath."], ["Which locator is best in Selenium?", "Prefer id, then a stable data-test attribute via CSS, then CSS selectors. XPath last, and only relative XPath."], ["What is the difference between CSS selector and XPath?", "CSS is faster and cleaner but only moves down the DOM. XPath can traverse up and use text, but is slower and more brittle."], ["What is absolute vs relative XPath?", "Absolute starts from the root (/html/body/...) and breaks on any structure change. Relative starts with // and targets attributes, so it survives layout changes."], ["What is an implicit wait?", "A global timeout that makes findElement poll until the element appears or the time runs out."], ["What is an explicit wait?", "WebDriverWait with an ExpectedCondition, waiting for a specific state like visibility or clickability of one element."], ["What is a fluent wait?", "An explicit wait where you also set the polling interval and which exceptions to ignore."], ["Should I mix implicit and explicit waits?", "No. Mixing them causes unpredictable timeouts. Use explicit waits only."], ["What is Thread.sleep and why avoid it?", "A hard pause. It slows suites and still fails when the app is slower than the pause. Use explicit waits instead."], ["What is the Page Object Model?", "A design pattern where each page or component is a class holding its locators and actions. Tests read like business steps and locator changes are fixed in one place."], ["What is Page Factory?", "A Selenium helper that initialises page object elements with @FindBy annotations and lazy loading."], ["How do you handle dropdowns in Selenium?", "For HTML select elements use the Select class (selectByVisibleText, selectByValue, selectByIndex). For custom dropdowns, click and pick the option element."], ["How do you handle alerts in Selenium?", "driver.switchTo().alert() then accept(), dismiss(), getText() or sendKeys()."], ["How do you handle frames in Selenium?", "driver.switchTo().frame(index, name or element), work inside, then switchTo().defaultContent()."], ["How do you handle multiple windows or tabs?", "Use getWindowHandles(), switch to the new handle with switchTo().window(handle), and switch back after."], ["How do you take a screenshot in Selenium?", "Cast the driver to TakesScreenshot and call getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE). Attach on failure in a listener."], ["How do you upload a file in Selenium?", "Send the file path with sendKeys() to the input type=file element. Avoid the OS dialog."], ["How do you handle a file download in Selenium?", "Set browser preferences for the download directory and disable prompts, then verify the file exists on disk."], ["What is StaleElementReferenceException?", "The element you held was re-rendered or removed. Re-find the element, or wait for the DOM to settle."], ["What is NoSuchElementException?", "The locator matched nothing at that moment. Check the locator, wait for the element, or check you are in the right frame or window."], ["What is ElementClickInterceptedException?", "Another element (overlay, toast) covers the target. Wait for it to disappear, scroll into view, or use JavaScript click as a last resort."], ["How do you scroll in Selenium?", "Use JavascriptExecutor with window.scrollBy or scrollIntoView, or the Actions class scrollToElement in Selenium 4."], ["What is JavascriptExecutor?", "An interface to run JavaScript in the browser from Selenium, used for scrolling, reading hidden values or clicking when normal clicks fail."], ["What is the Actions class?", "Selenium's API for complex gestures: hover, drag and drop, right click, double click, key combinations."], ["How do you handle dynamic elements?", "Use stable attributes, partial matches (contains, starts-with) and explicit waits. Ask developers for data-test ids."], ["What is headless testing in Selenium?", "Running the browser without a UI (Chrome or Firefox headless). Faster and CI friendly, but validate visual behaviour occasionally with a headed run."], ["What is the difference between driver.close() and driver.quit()?", "close() closes the current window. quit() closes all windows and ends the driver session."], ["What is the difference between findElement and findElements?", "findElement returns one element or throws. findElements returns a list, empty if nothing matches."], ["How do you verify text in Selenium?", "Read getText() and assert with your test framework. Trim and normalise whitespace before comparing."], ["How do you handle authentication pop-ups?", "Pass credentials in the URL (user:pass@host) where allowed, or use a proxy or browser profile. Avoid OS dialogs."], ["How do you run Selenium tests in parallel?", "Use the test framework (TestNG parallel, pytest-xdist) with thread-safe driver handling, or Selenium Grid."], ["What is TestNG in Selenium projects?", "A Java test framework providing annotations, grouping, parallel execution, data providers and reports, commonly paired with Selenium."], ["How do you do data-driven testing in Selenium?", "Feed data from Excel, CSV, JSON or a database into the test through DataProvider (TestNG) or parametrize (pytest)."], ["What is Selenium 4's relative locators?", "Locators like above(), below(), toLeftOf(), near() that find elements by position relative to a known element."], ["What changed in Selenium 4?", "W3C WebDriver standard by default, relative locators, new window and tab APIs, Chrome DevTools Protocol access, and an improved Grid."], ["How do you integrate Selenium with CI?", "Run headless in Jenkins or GitHub Actions on every push, publish reports (Allure, Extent) and fail the build on test failures."], ["How do you handle flaky Selenium tests?", "Replace sleeps with explicit waits, isolate test data, stabilise locators, retry only as a last resort and track flaky rate."], ["How do you handle cookies in Selenium?", "manage().getCookies(), addCookie(), deleteCookieNamed(). Useful to skip login by injecting a session cookie."], ["How do you handle CAPTCHA in Selenium?", "You do not automate CAPTCHA. Ask for a test environment flag, a bypass key, or a test account without it."], ["Can Selenium test mobile apps?", "No. Use Appium for native apps. Selenium can drive mobile browsers through Appium or device emulation."], ["Can Selenium test desktop applications?", "No. Use WinAppDriver, Pywinauto or similar tools for desktop."], ["What is a WebElement?", "The object representing a DOM element in Selenium, exposing click, sendKeys, getText, getAttribute and similar methods."], ["How do you get an attribute value in Selenium?", "element.getAttribute(\"href\") or getDomAttribute and getDomProperty in Selenium 4."], ["What is the best structure for a Selenium framework?", "Layers: tests, page objects, utilities (waits, driver factory, config), test data, and reporting. Keep tests free of locators."], ["How do you report Selenium results?", "Allure or Extent Reports with screenshots on failure, plus the CI dashboard for trends."], ["What is the difference between Selenium and Playwright?", "Playwright has built-in auto-waits, network control and multi-browser support from one API. Selenium has wider language support and a larger ecosystem. Both are solid choices."], ["What is the difference between Selenium and Cypress?", "Cypress runs inside the browser, JavaScript only, great developer experience, limited multi-tab and cross-origin. Selenium drives real browsers externally in many languages."]], "Appium": [["What is Appium?", "Appium is an open-source automation tool for native, hybrid and mobile web apps on Android and iOS, using the WebDriver protocol."], ["How does Appium work?", "Your test sends WebDriver commands to the Appium server, which translates them for the platform driver (UiAutomator2 on Android, XCUITest on iOS) that controls the device."], ["What are Appium drivers?", "Platform-specific engines: UiAutomator2 and Espresso for Android, XCUITest for iOS, plus drivers for Windows, Mac and Flutter."], ["What is UiAutomator2?", "Google's Android UI testing framework, used by Appium's default Android driver."], ["What is XCUITest?", "Apple's UI testing framework, used by Appium's iOS driver. Requires macOS and Xcode."], ["What are desired capabilities?", "Key-value settings that tell Appium what to launch: platformName, deviceName, app path or bundle id, automationName, and options like noReset."], ["What is automationName?", "The capability that selects the driver, for example UiAutomator2 or XCUITest."], ["What is the difference between app, appPackage and appActivity?", "app is the APK or IPA path to install. appPackage and appActivity launch an already installed Android app by package and starting activity."], ["What is noReset and fullReset?", "noReset keeps app data between sessions. fullReset uninstalls and reinstalls the app. Choose based on whether the test needs a clean state."], ["Which locators does Appium support?", "id (resource-id or accessibility id), accessibility id, className, xpath, Android UiAutomator selectors, iOS predicate strings and class chains."], ["Which locator is best in Appium?", "Accessibility id first, it works on both platforms and is stable. Then resource-id on Android, predicate strings on iOS. XPath last."], ["What is Appium Inspector?", "A desktop tool to view the app's element tree, try locators and record actions."], ["How do you find elements on iOS in Appium?", "Accessibility id, iOS predicate string (label, name, value, type) or class chain for hierarchy queries."], ["How do you handle waits in Appium?", "Same as Selenium: WebDriverWait with expected conditions. Avoid implicit waits and sleeps."], ["How do you swipe or scroll in Appium?", "Use W3C Actions (pointer sequence) or mobile: commands like mobile: scroll and mobile: swipeGesture on Android."], ["How do you tap by coordinates in Appium?", "W3C Actions pointer move and down/up at x,y. Use only when no element locator exists."], ["How do you handle hybrid apps in Appium?", "Switch context: driver.getContextHandles(), switch to the WEBVIEW context for web content, back to NATIVE_APP for native screens."], ["How do you test mobile web with Appium?", "Set browserName to Chrome or Safari in capabilities and drive it like Selenium."], ["How do you handle permissions pop-ups in Appium?", "Use autoGrantPermissions (Android) and autoAcceptAlerts (iOS) capabilities, or handle the dialog explicitly."], ["How do you handle OTP in Appium tests?", "Use a test backend that returns a fixed code, read the SMS via adb on Android, or fetch it from a test inbox API. Never hard-code production OTPs."], ["How do you run Appium on real devices?", "Enable USB debugging (Android) or trust the developer certificate and set up WebDriverAgent signing (iOS). Use udid in capabilities."], ["How do you run Appium tests in parallel?", "Start multiple Appium servers or use Appium 2 with different ports and systemPort/wdaLocalPort, one device per session."], ["What is Appium 2 vs Appium 1?", "Appium 2 splits drivers and plugins into installable packages (appium driver install uiautomator2), removes bundled drivers and standardises on W3C."], ["How do you install Appium 2?", "npm install -g appium, then appium driver install uiautomator2 and xcuitest as needed."], ["What is Appium Doctor?", "A CLI tool that checks your environment: Java, Android SDK, Xcode, environment variables."], ["How do you rotate the device in Appium?", "driver.rotate(ScreenOrientation.LANDSCAPE) or the orientation capability."], ["How do you handle push notifications in Appium?", "On Android open the notification shade with openNotifications(). On iOS interact through the springboard or trigger via test hooks."], ["How do you background the app in Appium?", "driver.runAppInBackground(seconds) or terminateApp and activateApp for kill and relaunch."], ["How do you test deep links in Appium?", "Android: mobile: deepLink with url and package. iOS: launch Safari with the URL or use mobile: deepLink where supported."], ["How do you handle keyboard actions in Appium?", "sendKeys to the field, driver.hideKeyboard(), or pressKey with AndroidKey codes."], ["How do you take a screenshot in Appium?", "driver.getScreenshotAs(OutputType.FILE), and use screenRecording for videos."], ["How do you record video in Appium?", "startRecordingScreen() and stopRecordingScreen(), which returns a base64 mp4."], ["How do you check app installation in Appium?", "driver.isAppInstalled(bundleOrPackage), and installApp or removeApp to manage it."], ["How do you get device logs in Appium?", "driver.manage().logs().get(\"logcat\") on Android, \"syslog\" on iOS."], ["How do you handle toast messages in Appium?", "Use an XPath on android.widget.Toast with text, captured quickly with a short wait."], ["How do you handle date pickers in Appium?", "Prefer setting the value directly if the app allows, otherwise scroll pickers via UiScrollable or type into the field."], ["What is UiScrollable?", "An Android UiAutomator selector that scrolls until a matching element is found, in one command."], ["What is a Page Object Model in Appium?", "The same pattern as web: one class per screen with locators and actions, often with platform-specific locator sets behind one interface."], ["How do you handle both Android and iOS in one framework?", "Use accessibility ids shared across platforms where possible, and platform-conditional locators for the rest."], ["What are common Appium errors?", "Session not created (wrong capabilities or device offline), element not found (timing or context), WebDriverAgent build failures on iOS (signing)."], ["How do you speed up Appium tests?", "Use accessibility ids, avoid XPath, reuse sessions with noReset, run in parallel, and skip UI for setup by using APIs."], ["Can Appium test Flutter apps?", "Yes, with the appium-flutter-driver, or by adding semantics labels and using standard drivers."], ["Can Appium test React Native apps?", "Yes. Set testID props, which map to accessibility ids on both platforms."], ["How do you integrate Appium with CI?", "Run on device farms (BrowserStack, Sauce Labs, Firebase Test Lab) or self-hosted devices from Jenkins or GitHub Actions, with reports published per run."], ["What is a device farm?", "A cloud of real devices you rent for automated and manual testing, useful for OS and model coverage you do not own."], ["How do you test app upgrades with Appium?", "Install the old build, create data, installApp with the new build without reset, then verify data and flows survive."], ["How do you handle network conditions in Appium?", "On Android toggle with setConnection or adb commands; on iOS use Network Link Conditioner or a proxy like Charles."], ["How do you verify analytics events in mobile tests?", "Route the device through Charles Proxy or mitmproxy and assert on captured requests to Mixpanel, Adjust or your analytics endpoint."], ["What is Espresso and when to use it over Appium?", "Espresso is Android's native, fast, white-box framework run by developers. Appium is black-box and cross-platform, better for QA-owned end-to-end suites."], ["What is XCUITest vs Appium?", "XCUITest is Apple-native, Swift, fast but iOS only. Appium wraps it for cross-platform, multi-language testing."], ["How do you handle biometrics in Appium?", "Use simulator commands (mobile: enrollBiometric, mobile: sendBiometricMatch) or a test flag; real fingerprints cannot be automated."]], "Postman": [["What is Postman?", "Postman is an API platform for sending requests, organising them in collections, writing test scripts and running them manually, in CI or on a schedule."], ["What is a collection in Postman?", "A folder of related requests with shared auth, variables and scripts, exportable as JSON."], ["What are environments in Postman?", "Sets of variables (base URL, tokens) you switch between, for example dev, staging, production."], ["What are Postman variables and their scopes?", "Global, collection, environment, data (from runner files) and local. Narrowest scope wins."], ["How do you set a variable from a response?", "In the Tests tab: pm.environment.set(\"token\", pm.response.json().token) or pm.collectionVariables.set."], ["What is the Pre-request Script?", "JavaScript that runs before the request is sent, used to generate timestamps, signatures or set dynamic variables."], ["What is the Tests tab?", "JavaScript that runs after the response, where you write assertions with pm.test and pm.expect."], ["How do you assert a status code in Postman?", "pm.test(\"200\", () => pm.response.to.have.status(200));"], ["How do you assert JSON fields in Postman?", "const j = pm.response.json(); pm.expect(j.id).to.be.a(\"number\"); pm.expect(j.email).to.eql(\"a@b.com\");"], ["How do you validate a JSON schema in Postman?", "pm.response.to.have.jsonSchema(schema) using a JSON Schema object stored in a variable."], ["How do you check response time in Postman?", "pm.expect(pm.response.responseTime).to.be.below(500);"], ["What is the Collection Runner?", "A tool that runs every request in a collection in order, with iterations and data files, showing pass and fail per test."], ["What is Newman?", "Postman's CLI runner. It executes exported collections from the terminal or CI and produces reports (CLI, JSON, HTML, JUnit)."], ["How do you run Newman?", "newman run collection.json -e env.json -r cli,htmlextra"], ["How do you integrate Postman with CI?", "Export collection and environment, run Newman in Jenkins or GitHub Actions, publish the HTML or JUnit report, fail on assertion failures."], ["What is a Postman monitor?", "A scheduled cloud run of a collection that alerts you when tests fail, useful for uptime and contract checks."], ["How do you chain requests in Postman?", "Save values from one response into variables and use them in later requests, for example {{token}} or {{userId}}."], ["How do you handle authentication in Postman?", "Set it at collection level: Bearer token, Basic, API key, OAuth 2.0 with token refresh, or Digest."], ["How do you do OAuth 2.0 in Postman?", "Choose OAuth 2.0 in Authorization, fill client id, secret, auth and token URLs, get a token, and Postman attaches it to requests."], ["What is a mock server in Postman?", "A hosted endpoint that returns saved example responses, so front-end or tests can proceed before the real API exists."], ["What are examples in Postman?", "Saved request and response pairs attached to a request, used for documentation and mocks."], ["How do you document APIs in Postman?", "Add descriptions to requests and collections, save examples, then publish the collection as documentation."], ["How do you do data-driven tests in Postman?", "Run the collection with a CSV or JSON file in the Runner or Newman -d data.json; access rows via {{column}} or pm.iterationData.get."], ["How do you test negative cases in Postman?", "Send invalid bodies, missing auth and wrong types, and assert 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 with the right error message structure."], ["How do you test file uploads in Postman?", "Body as form-data, set the key type to File, select the file, and assert the response."], ["How do you send GraphQL requests in Postman?", "Choose the GraphQL body type, paste the query and variables, and assert on data and errors."], ["How do you test WebSockets in Postman?", "Create a WebSocket request, connect, send messages and inspect received frames."], ["How do you handle dynamic values like timestamps?", "Use built-in variables {{$timestamp}}, {{$guid}}, {{$randomInt}}, or generate them in a pre-request script."], ["What is pm.sendRequest?", "A function to make an extra HTTP call inside scripts, for example to fetch a token before the main request."], ["How do you skip a request in a run?", "postman.setNextRequest(\"Request name\") jumps, and postman.setNextRequest(null) stops the run."], ["How do you share collections with a team?", "Use a Postman workspace, or export JSON into version control alongside the code."], ["How do you version collections?", "Fork and merge in Postman, or keep exported JSON in Git with pull requests."], ["How do you compare API responses across environments?", "Run the same collection against each environment and compare the Newman reports, or write assertions against expected data per environment."], ["How do you test rate limiting in Postman?", "Run a request many iterations quickly and assert a 429 appears with Retry-After."], ["How do you test pagination in Postman?", "Assert page size, next tokens or links, total counts, and that the last page returns no next pointer."], ["How do you handle cookies in Postman?", "The cookie jar stores them per domain automatically; view and edit under Cookies, or read them with pm.cookies."], ["How do you test headers in Postman?", "pm.response.to.have.header(\"Content-Type\", \"application/json\");"], ["What is the difference between Postman and Newman?", "Postman is the GUI app; Newman is the CLI that runs Postman collections for automation."], ["What is the difference between Postman and Insomnia?", "Both are API clients. Postman has stronger collaboration, monitors and test runner; Insomnia is lighter and popular for GraphQL."], ["What is the difference between Postman and REST Assured?", "Postman is a low-code tool for exploration and CI runs. REST Assured is a Java library for API tests inside a code framework."], ["How do you organise a large Postman collection?", "Folders by feature or endpoint, collection-level auth and variables, shared scripts at collection level, naming convention Method Resource Case."], ["How do you reuse scripts across requests?", "Put common functions in collection-level pre-request and test scripts, or store them as strings in variables and eval them."], ["How do you validate error message format?", "Assert the error object schema (code, message, details) is consistent across all failure responses."], ["How do you test an API that needs a signature?", "Compute the HMAC in a pre-request script with CryptoJS and set it as a header variable."], ["How do you test idempotency?", "Send the same POST twice with the same idempotency key and assert only one resource is created."], ["How do you check for sensitive data leaks in responses?", "Assert that fields like password, token or full card number are absent: pm.expect(j).to.not.have.property(\"password\")."], ["How do you export a collection?", "Collection menu, Export, choose v2.1 JSON."], ["What is Postman Flows?", "A visual, low-code canvas to chain API calls and logic into workflows."], ["How do you set a timeout in Newman?", "--timeout-request 5000 sets a per-request timeout in milliseconds."], ["How do you handle SSL errors in Newman?", "--insecure disables certificate validation for test environments only."], ["What is contract testing with Postman?", "Asserting responses match an agreed schema so backend changes that break clients are caught early."]], "Jira": [["What is Jira?", "Jira is Atlassian's issue tracking and project management tool, used for bugs, tasks, stories and agile boards."], ["What are issue types in Jira?", "Epic, Story, Task, Bug and Sub-task by default; teams add custom types like Test or Improvement."], ["What is an Epic in Jira?", "A large body of work broken into stories and tasks, tracked across sprints."], ["What is a Story in Jira?", "A user-facing requirement small enough to finish in a sprint, usually with acceptance criteria."], ["What is a Bug in Jira?", "An issue type for defects, with fields for severity, priority, environment and steps to reproduce."], ["What is a Sub-task?", "A smaller piece of a parent issue, for example \"Write test cases\" under a story."], ["What is a Jira workflow?", "The set of statuses and transitions an issue moves through, for example To Do, In Progress, In Review, QA, Done."], ["What is a good QA workflow in Jira?", "Add statuses like Ready for QA, In QA, QA Failed and QA Passed, with transitions that require a comment or a linked defect."], ["What is a Jira board?", "A visual view of issues: Scrum boards with sprints and backlog, Kanban boards with continuous flow and WIP limits."], ["What is the difference between Scrum and Kanban boards?", "Scrum plans work in fixed sprints with velocity tracking. Kanban is continuous with WIP limits and cycle time."], ["What is a sprint in Jira?", "A time-boxed iteration (usually two weeks) with a committed set of issues."], ["What is the backlog?", "The ordered list of issues not yet in a sprint, refined and prioritised by the product owner."], ["What is JQL?", "Jira Query Language, used to filter issues, for example project = APP AND type = Bug AND status != Done ORDER BY priority DESC."], ["Give a JQL for open bugs assigned to me.", "assignee = currentUser() AND type = Bug AND resolution = Unresolved"], ["Give a JQL for bugs created this sprint.", "type = Bug AND sprint in openSprints() AND created >= startOfWeek(-1)"], ["What is a Jira filter?", "A saved JQL query you can share, subscribe to and use in dashboards and boards."], ["What is a Jira dashboard?", "A page of gadgets (filter results, pie charts, created vs resolved) for tracking quality and progress."], ["What dashboards should QA have?", "Open bugs by severity, bugs by component, created vs resolved trend, reopened bugs, and blockers for the current release."], ["What is priority vs severity in Jira?", "Priority is a default field for fix order. Severity is usually a custom field for impact. Keep both and define levels in a wiki."], ["How do you link issues in Jira?", "Use issue links: blocks, is blocked by, relates to, duplicates, and \"tests\" links when using a test management app."], ["What are components in Jira?", "Sub-sections of a project (Login, Payments, eSIM) used to route and report issues."], ["What are labels in Jira?", "Free-form tags for cross-cutting grouping like regression, automation-candidate or release-blocker."], ["What are versions or fixVersions?", "Release identifiers. fixVersion marks which release contains the fix; affectsVersion marks where the bug was found."], ["What is a release in Jira?", "A version with a release date and a page showing done and unresolved issues for that build."], ["What is a Jira epic vs a version?", "Epic groups by feature; version groups by release. An epic can span versions."], ["How do you write a good bug in Jira?", "Clear title, environment, steps, expected vs actual, severity, priority, attachments, component and affected version."], ["What are Jira automation rules?", "No-code rules that trigger on events, for example move the parent story to In QA when all sub-tasks are done, or notify Slack on a new blocker."], ["How do you integrate Jira with Confluence?", "Link pages to issues, embed JQL tables in pages, and create issues from Confluence text."], ["How do you integrate Jira with Slack?", "The Jira Cloud Slack app posts issue updates to channels and lets you create issues from messages."], ["How do you integrate Jira with test management?", "Apps like Xray, Zephyr or TestRail link test cases and executions to stories and defects."], ["What is Xray?", "A Jira app adding test, test set, test plan and execution issue types with traceability and reports."], ["What is Zephyr?", "A Jira test management app for test cases, cycles and execution tracking."], ["How do you track test progress in Jira?", "Use a test management app, or a Test issue type with status per case and a dashboard gadget per cycle."], ["What is story points estimation?", "Relative sizing of effort and complexity, used for velocity, not hours."], ["What is velocity?", "The average story points completed per sprint, used for forecasting."], ["What is a burndown chart?", "A chart showing remaining work in a sprint against time."], ["What is a burnup chart?", "A chart showing completed work against total scope, so scope changes are visible."], ["What is the control chart in Jira?", "A Kanban report showing cycle time per issue and the rolling average."], ["What is the cumulative flow diagram?", "A chart of issue counts per status over time, exposing bottlenecks."], ["What is Definition of Done?", "The team's agreed checklist for an issue to be Done, usually including tests passed and no open critical bugs."], ["What is acceptance criteria and where does it go in Jira?", "Conditions a story must meet; put them in a dedicated field or a fixed section in the description so QA tests against them."], ["How do you handle reopened bugs in Jira?", "A Reopened status or transition back to In Progress with a required comment, and a dashboard tracking reopen rate."], ["How do you triage bugs in Jira?", "A regular meeting reviewing new bugs, confirming severity and priority, assigning owners and fixVersions."], ["What is a Jira service project?", "Jira Service Management for support tickets with SLAs, separate from software projects."], ["What is the difference between Jira Software and Jira Work Management?", "Software is for dev teams with agile boards; Work Management is for business teams with lists and calendars."], ["How do you bulk edit issues in Jira?", "Search with JQL, choose Bulk change, pick edit, transition or move, and apply to all results."], ["How do you export from Jira?", "Export search results to CSV or Excel from the issue navigator."], ["What are Jira permissions and roles?", "Permission schemes define who can create, edit, transition or resolve; roles like Administrators, Developers and QA map users to them."], ["How do you handle duplicate bugs in Jira?", "Resolve as Duplicate with a link to the original; check with JQL text search before creating."], ["What is a Jira epic report?", "A report showing an epic's progress: done, in progress and to-do issues and estimates."], ["How do you connect Jira to CI?", "Jira integrations with Jenkins, GitHub or Bitbucket show builds and deployments on issues via smart commits."], ["What are smart commits?", "Commit messages like APP-123 #comment fixed #time 1h #done that update Jira issues automatically."]], "Trello": [["What is Trello?", "Trello is a Kanban-style board tool from Atlassian using boards, lists and cards to track work visually."], ["What are boards, lists and cards?", "A board is the project, lists are columns (stages), and cards are the individual tasks or bugs."], ["How do you use Trello for QA?", "Lists like Backlog, Ready for Test, In Test, Failed, Passed, Done, with one card per feature or bug and checklists for test steps."], ["What is a Trello checklist?", "A list of sub-items on a card, useful for test steps or acceptance criteria with progress shown as a percentage."], ["What are Trello labels?", "Coloured tags on cards for priority, severity, platform or type."], ["What are Power-Ups?", "Integrations added to a board, for example Calendar, Custom Fields, Jira, Slack, GitHub or Voting."], ["What is Butler in Trello?", "Trello's automation: rules, buttons and scheduled commands, for example move a card to Done when all checklist items are complete."], ["How do you set due dates in Trello?", "Each card has a due date with reminders; the Calendar Power-Up shows them on a calendar."], ["How do you assign members in Trello?", "Add members to a card; they get notifications and the card shows their avatar."], ["How do you track bugs in Trello?", "One card per bug with a template description (steps, expected, actual), labels for severity, attachments for evidence and a list per status."], ["What is a card template in Trello?", "A card marked as template that new cards copy, ideal for a standard bug report format."], ["How do you link Trello to Jira?", "With the Jira Power-Up you can attach Jira issues to cards and see status."], ["How do you link Trello to Slack?", "The Slack Power-Up posts card updates and lets you create cards from Slack."], ["How do you link Trello to GitHub?", "The GitHub Power-Up attaches branches, commits and pull requests to cards."], ["What are custom fields in Trello?", "Extra fields on cards (dropdown, number, date, text) via the Custom Fields Power-Up, for severity or build number."], ["What is the difference between Trello and Jira?", "Trello is simpler and visual for small teams. Jira has workflows, JQL, reports and agile tooling for engineering teams."], ["How do you archive cards in Trello?", "Card menu, Archive. Archived cards can be searched and restored."], ["How do you archive a list in Trello?", "List menu, Archive this list."], ["How do you move cards between boards?", "Card menu, Move, pick board and list."], ["How do you copy a board?", "Board menu, More, Copy board, choose whether to keep cards."], ["What is the Trello board menu?", "The side panel for filters, Power-Ups, automation, activity log and settings."], ["How do you filter cards in Trello?", "Use the filter to show cards by label, member, due date or keyword."], ["What is the activity log?", "The board and card history of changes, comments and moves."], ["How do you comment on a card?", "Add a comment in the card; use @mentions to notify members."], ["How do you attach files in Trello?", "Attach from computer, Google Drive, Dropbox or a link directly on the card."], ["What is a Trello workspace?", "A group of boards and members for a team or company."], ["What are Trello views?", "Board, Timeline, Table, Calendar, Dashboard and Map views (paid plans) of the same cards."], ["What is the Timeline view?", "A Gantt-style view of cards by start and due date."], ["What is the Dashboard view?", "Charts of cards per list, per member, per label and due dates."], ["How do you set WIP limits in Trello?", "Use the List Limits Power-Up to highlight lists over a card count."], ["How do you run a sprint in Trello?", "Lists per sprint or a Sprint label, with Butler moving cards and a dashboard for progress."], ["How do you manage a test cycle in Trello?", "A board per release, a card per test area with checklists per case, labels for pass or fail and a Failed list linking to bug cards."], ["How do you prioritise in Trello?", "Order cards top to bottom in the list and use priority labels."], ["How do you handle recurring tasks in Trello?", "Butler scheduled commands or the Card Repeater Power-Up."], ["How do you export a Trello board?", "Board menu, Print and export, JSON (free) or CSV (paid)."], ["How do you set card cover images?", "Attach an image and make it the cover, or pick a colour cover for quick visual status."], ["How do you use Trello for a QA roadmap?", "Lists per quarter, cards per initiative (automation, process, tooling) with checklists for milestones."], ["How do you use Trello for release checklists?", "A card per release with a checklist: regression passed, sign-off, release notes, store submission, monitoring."], ["What are Trello stickers and emojis?", "Visual markers on cards; use sparingly for blockers or approvals."], ["How do you keep a Trello board clean?", "Archive Done cards weekly, limit labels to a defined set, and use templates for consistency."], ["What are Trello shortcuts?", "Press Q to show your cards, F to filter, B to open boards, and hover a card and press L for labels or D for due date."], ["How do you get notifications in Trello?", "Watch cards, lists or boards; notifications appear in-app and by email."], ["How does Trello handle permissions?", "Board visibility (private, workspace, public) and member roles (admin, normal, observer)."], ["What is an observer in Trello?", "A member who can view and comment but not edit cards."], ["Can Trello do dependencies?", "Not natively; use card links, checklists with card references or a Power-Up like Hello Epics."], ["How do you report from Trello?", "Dashboard view, or export CSV to Excel for custom charts."], ["How do you use Trello with Butler to notify on failures?", "Rule: when a card moves to Failed, add the QA lead as member and post to Slack."], ["What is Trello Enterprise?", "A plan with organisation-wide admin controls, security and unlimited workspaces."], ["Is Trello good for large QA teams?", "It works for small to mid teams; large teams usually need Jira or a test management tool for traceability."], ["How do you migrate from Trello to Jira?", "Jira's importer maps lists to statuses and cards to issues, or export CSV and import."]], "Test cases": [["What is a test case?", "A documented set of preconditions, steps, data and expected results used to verify a specific behaviour."], ["What are the fields of a test case?", "ID, title, module, precondition, steps, test data, expected result, actual result, status, priority, and links to requirements."], ["What is a test scenario vs a test case?", "A scenario is a high-level statement of what to test (verify login). Test cases are the detailed steps that cover the scenario."], ["What is a test suite?", "A group of test cases organised by feature, type or release."], ["What makes a good test case?", "One clear objective, atomic steps, exact expected results, reusable data, traceable to a requirement, understandable by a new tester."], ["How do you write a test case title?", "Verb, object, condition: \"Verify login fails with expired password\"."], ["How many steps should a test case have?", "Enough to reproduce without guessing, usually 3 to 10. Longer means split it."], ["What is a precondition?", "The state required before starting, for example \"user is registered and logged out\"."], ["What is expected result?", "The exact observable outcome: message text, screen, status code, data change."], ["What is a positive test case?", "Valid inputs and normal flow, verifying the feature works as intended."], ["What is a negative test case?", "Invalid inputs, missing data, wrong order, verifying the system handles errors gracefully."], ["What is an edge case?", "Inputs at or beyond limits: empty, maximum length, zero, negative, special characters, leap dates."], ["What is a happy path?", "The main success flow with valid data, no errors."], ["How do you prioritise test cases?", "By business impact, usage frequency, risk of failure and regulatory need. Mark P1 to P3."], ["What is a test case template?", "A standard format so every case has the same fields, in Excel, TestRail, Xray or Confluence."], ["How do you write test cases from requirements?", "Break each requirement into conditions, write positive, negative and boundary cases for each, and map them in a traceability matrix."], ["How do you write test cases from a Figma design?", "Walk each screen and state: default, loading, empty, error, success, permission denied, and interactions on each element."], ["How do you write test cases for login?", "Valid login, wrong password, wrong email, empty fields, locked account, password visibility toggle, remember me, session expiry, OTP or 2FA paths."], ["How do you write test cases for payment?", "Success, declined, insufficient funds, 3DS pass and fail, timeout, duplicate submit, refund, currency and rounding, receipt content."], ["How do you write test cases for OTP?", "Correct code, wrong code, expired code, resend limits, multiple devices, copy-paste, autofill, and delivery time."], ["How do you write test cases for a search feature?", "Exact match, partial, no results, special characters, spacing, case sensitivity, pagination, sorting and response time."], ["How do you write test cases for a form?", "Each field: required, format, length, boundary values, error messages, tab order, submit with mixed valid and invalid."], ["What is equivalence partitioning in test cases?", "Group inputs that behave the same and pick one representative per group."], ["What is boundary value analysis in test cases?", "Test at min, min-1, min+1, max, max-1, max+1."], ["What is a decision table?", "A grid of conditions and actions used to derive cases for complex business rules."], ["What is state transition testing?", "Cases derived from valid and invalid state changes, for example order states."], ["What is a use case vs a test case?", "A use case describes how an actor achieves a goal. Test cases verify each path of that use case."], ["How do you review test cases?", "Peer review against requirements: coverage, clarity, duplicates, correct expected results, and data validity."], ["How do you maintain test cases?", "Update on every requirement change, archive obsolete ones, tag automated ones, and version them with releases."], ["How do you organise test cases?", "By module then feature, with tags for type (smoke, regression, security) and platform."], ["What is a regression test case set?", "The subset of cases covering core flows, rerun on every release."], ["What is a smoke test case set?", "A handful of critical cases confirming the build is testable."], ["How do you estimate test case execution time?", "Average minutes per case by complexity multiplied by count, plus setup and defect logging overhead."], ["What is test case traceability?", "Linking each case to its requirement and each defect to its case, usually in an RTM."], ["What tools store test cases?", "TestRail, Xray, Zephyr, qTest, PractiTest, or Excel and Confluence for small teams."], ["How do you write test cases for APIs?", "Per endpoint: valid request, each validation error, auth failures, not found, schema check, response time."], ["How do you write test cases for notifications?", "Trigger conditions, content, deep link on tap, app states (foreground, background, killed), permission off, badge count."], ["How do you write test cases for deep links?", "Installed app opens correct screen, not installed goes to store then correct screen (deferred), logged out redirects then returns, invalid link handling."], ["How do you write test cases for analytics?", "Each event: trigger, name, properties, user identity, timing, no duplicates, appears in dashboard."], ["How do you write test cases for localisation?", "Translated strings, layout with long text, date and number formats, RTL where relevant."], ["How do you write test cases for accessibility?", "Screen reader labels, focus order, contrast, touch target size, keyboard navigation."], ["How do you write test cases for performance?", "Response under load thresholds, screen load time, memory and battery on mobile."], ["What is exploratory testing vs test cases?", "Exploratory uses charters instead of steps, finding what scripted cases miss. Use both."], ["Can AI write test cases?", "Yes, from requirements or Figma, and it saves hours. Every generated case still needs a human review for correctness and relevance."], ["What is test data in a test case?", "The exact inputs used, kept separate from steps so cases can be reused with other data."], ["How do you handle test cases for multiple platforms?", "One case with platform-specific expected results, or tags per platform in the tool."], ["What is pass, fail, blocked, skipped?", "Pass: expected met. Fail: not met. Blocked: cannot run due to dependency or bug. Skipped: intentionally not run this cycle."], ["How do you report test case results?", "Executed vs planned, pass rate, failed cases with linked defects, blocked reasons, and coverage of requirements."], ["What is a test case ID convention?", "Project-Module-Number, for example APP-LOG-014, stable across versions."], ["How detailed should steps be?", "Detailed enough for a new tester to follow without asking, not so detailed that UI text changes break them."]], "Confluence": [["What is Confluence?", "Confluence is Atlassian's wiki and documentation tool for team spaces, pages and knowledge bases."], ["What is a Confluence space?", "A container for related pages, for example a QA space or a project space."], ["What is a page tree?", "The hierarchy of parent and child pages in a space."], ["What should a QA space contain?", "Test strategy, test plans per release, test case libraries or links, bug reporting guidelines, environments, tools setup, release reports, and onboarding."], ["How do you write a test plan in Confluence?", "Use a template: scope, approach, environments, schedule, roles, risks, entry and exit criteria, and embedded Jira filters for tracking."], ["What are Confluence templates?", "Pre-built page structures (meeting notes, retrospective, product requirements) that you can customise for QA."], ["How do you embed Jira in Confluence?", "Use the Jira Issues macro with a JQL query; it shows a live table of issues."], ["What is the Jira Issues macro?", "A macro rendering a filtered list of Jira issues inside a page, refreshed live."], ["How do you create a Jira issue from Confluence?", "Highlight text and choose Create Jira issue, the link is added to the page."], ["What are Confluence macros?", "Blocks that add content: table of contents, status, expand, code, panel, roadmap, charts, Jira issues."], ["What is the Status macro?", "A coloured lozenge (In Progress, Done, Blocked) for quick visual state."], ["What is the Expand macro?", "A collapsible section, useful for long logs or detailed steps."], ["What is the Table of Contents macro?", "Auto-generated links to the page's headings."], ["How do you version pages in Confluence?", "Every save creates a version; Page History lets you compare and restore."], ["How do you comment in Confluence?", "Inline comments on selected text, or page comments at the bottom."], ["How do you restrict a page?", "Page restrictions for view and edit by user or group."], ["How do you organise release documentation?", "A parent page per release with children: plan, test results, known issues, sign-off."], ["What is a release sign-off page?", "A page recording scope tested, pass rate, open defects with risk, and approvals from QA, product and engineering."], ["How do you document a bug reporting guideline?", "A page with the template, severity and priority definitions, examples of good and bad reports, and evidence requirements."], ["How do you document test environments?", "URLs, credentials location (not the credentials), data reset steps, third-party sandbox details and owners."], ["How do you write a QA onboarding page?", "Tools access checklist, process overview, key documents, who to ask, and first-week tasks."], ["How do you document test cases in Confluence?", "Tables per feature or the Test Case macro from an app; for larger suites link to TestRail or Xray."], ["What is a requirement traceability matrix in Confluence?", "A table mapping requirement IDs to test case IDs and Jira defects, or a Jira Issues macro per requirement."], ["How do you use labels in Confluence?", "Tag pages (qa, release-2-4, telecom) so Content by Label macros can pull them into index pages."], ["What is the Content by Label macro?", "Lists pages with given labels, useful for an auto-updating index of test plans."], ["How do you make a QA dashboard page?", "Jira Issues macros for open bugs, chart macros for trends, status lozenges for release readiness."], ["How do you export a Confluence page?", "Export to PDF or Word from the page menu; spaces can export to HTML or PDF."], ["How do you handle diagrams in Confluence?", "draw.io or Gliffy apps for flow diagrams, or embed images from Figma or Lucidchart."], ["How do you write meeting notes in Confluence?", "Meeting notes template with attendees, agenda, decisions and action items linked to Jira."], ["How do you document a test strategy?", "Objectives, test levels and types, automation approach, tools, environments, defect process, metrics, risks, and review cadence."], ["How do you document an API in Confluence?", "Endpoint tables with method, path, params, responses, and a link to the Postman collection or Swagger."], ["How do you document release notes for QA?", "Features tested, fixed bugs verified, known issues, test coverage summary, and build numbers."], ["What is page watching?", "Subscribing to a page or space to receive change notifications."], ["How do you search in Confluence?", "Global search with filters by space, contributor, type, label and date; use quotes for exact phrases."], ["How do you link pages?", "Type / to insert a link or use the link tool; anchors link to specific headings."], ["What is a Confluence blog post?", "A dated post in a space, useful for release announcements or QA newsletters."], ["How do you use Confluence for retrospectives?", "The retrospective template with what went well, what to improve and actions linked to Jira."], ["How do you archive pages?", "Move to an Archive parent or use the archive feature; keep the tree clean for current work."], ["How do you manage documentation quality?", "Owners per page, review dates, a status lozenge for Current or Outdated, and quarterly clean-ups."], ["How do you integrate Confluence with Slack?", "The Confluence Slack app posts page updates and mentions to channels."], ["What is the difference between Confluence and Notion?", "Confluence integrates deeply with Jira and is enterprise oriented; Notion is more flexible with databases and a lighter feel."], ["What is the difference between Confluence and Google Docs?", "Confluence organises knowledge in spaces with macros and Jira links; Docs is better for ad hoc collaborative writing."], ["How do you track document approvals?", "Use a status lozenge and an approvals table, or an app like Comala Document Management."], ["How do you handle images and screenshots?", "Upload directly; use the Gallery macro for multiple and annotate before upload."], ["How do you present test results in Confluence?", "A summary table (planned, executed, passed, failed, blocked), a chart macro, and a linked Jira filter for failures."], ["How do you document known issues?", "A table with Jira key, summary, workaround, impact and target fix version."], ["How do you write a definition of done page?", "A checklist agreed by the team, referenced from every story and reviewed each quarter."], ["How do you use Confluence for test data documentation?", "Data sets, how to generate or reset them, and ownership; never store real customer data."], ["What is a Confluence Cloud vs Data Center?", "Cloud is hosted by Atlassian; Data Center is self-hosted for large enterprises."], ["How do you keep Confluence from becoming a graveyard?", "Fewer pages, clear owners, index pages, labels, review dates, and delete or archive aggressively."]]}};